@* The genuine first-run state: nothing to connect to. Names what Dray looked for rather than
   saying "no engine found" and leaving the user to guess (PRODUCT.md: teach, don't shrug). *@

<EmptyState Icon="IconRef.Host"
            Title="No container engine found"
            Body="@LookedFor">
    <Actions>
        <Button Label="Look again" Icon="IconRef.Refresh" Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary" OnClick="OnRetry" />
        <Button Label="Add a host…" Icon="IconRef.Plus" />
    </Actions>
</EmptyState>

@if (OperatingSystem.IsWindows())
{
    @* Windows is the one platform where "install an engine" is two different answers, and a user
       who does not know that will install the wrong one. The two kinds of container do not share
       images, containers, volumes or networks, and nothing in Docker's own first-run explains it.
       Dray does not install either — it is a client (PRODUCT.md) — but it can say what the choice
       actually is. *@
    <div class="panel noengine__windows">
        <div class="panel__head"><span class="panel__title">Two kinds of engine on Windows</span></div>
        <div class="panel__body">
            <dl class="noengine__options">
                <dt>Linux containers</dt>
                <dd>
                    Need a Linux kernel, so they run inside a virtual machine. WSL2 is the usual one:
                    Docker Desktop, Rancher Desktop and <span class="mono">podman machine</span> each
                    set one up, or you can install an engine inside a distro yourself and Dray will
                    offer that distro as a host. This is what almost every image on Docker Hub is
                    built for.
                </dd>

                <dt>Windows containers</dt>
                <dd>
                    Run directly on this machine's own kernel — no WSL2 and no Linux virtual machine.
                    Windows ships the <span class="mono">Containers</span> feature that makes them
                    possible, but not an engine to drive it, so one still has to be installed: Docker
                    Desktop switched into Windows-container mode, or on Windows Server, Microsoft's
                    <span class="mono">install-docker-ce.ps1</span> script. On Windows 11 this needs
                    Pro or Enterprise with Hyper-V enabled. These are the images Microsoft publishes
                    — Nano Server, Server Core, and the .NET Framework images built on them.
                </dd>
            </dl>

            <p class="noengine__note muted">
                The two are separate engines with separate images, containers, volumes and networks.
                Switching between them does not convert anything; it changes which engine you are
                looking at.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnRetry { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What Dray actually looked at, named rather than summarised — a user who can see the list
    /// can tell whether the thing they installed is on it.
    /// </summary>
    static string LookedFor => OperatingSystem.IsWindows()
        ? "Dray looked at your Docker contexts, DOCKER_HOST, the named pipes Docker Desktop, a native Docker engine and Podman use, and your WSL2 distributions. Start one of those, or add a host to connect over SSH."
        : "Dray looked at your Docker contexts, DOCKER_HOST, and the sockets Docker Desktop, OrbStack, Colima, Rancher Desktop and Podman use. Start one of those, or add a host to connect over SSH.";
}
